The only pole reversal that has occured, or is likely ever to
occur, is magnetic pole reversal.  And even without magnetic
pole reversal, the magnetic poles wander around a bit.  Enough
so that they aren't used for anything requiring a great deal of
precision, like satellites and GPS.  The reversal (like the
current wandering) will doubtlessly affect pleasure boating and
the boy scouts, but it shouldn't affect professional navigation
(which is all inertial based or GPS today) to any great degree.

The most significant effect will be the fact that during the
reversal, the magnetic lines of force won't protect us nearly as
much from cosmic radiation and such.  The effect doesn't seem to
have been important in the past reversals, but maybe biological
systems are more robust to such things than, say, PC's and cell
phones.  (Or maybe the effect is offset by other effects.)

